.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The messages of the ssh\-agent protocol (draft\-ietf\-sshm\-ssh\-agent, formerly draft\-miller\-ssh\-agent), for a client.
.PP
A client can keep its private keys in an agent: auth.h hands the application the key, the algorithm and the bytes to sign (the sign event, and gf_auth_client_sign_respond or gf_session_auth_sign_respond to answer).  This is the agent's side of that: the framing, the requests a client needs and the replies it reads.  It does no I/O.  The application owns the socket (usually a Unix socket named by SSH_AUTH_SOCK), writes the bytes these functions make and gives what it reads to the reader.
.PP
A client asks for two things:
.IP \(bu 3
the identities the agent holds: gf_agent_request_identities, and gf_agent_identities on the answer;
.IP \(bu 3
a signature by one of them: gf_agent_sign_request, and gf_agent_sign_response on the answer, whose signature blob goes straight to the sign event's answer.
.PP
and, for an agent that holds keys restricted to certain destinations (ssh\-add \-h), tells it which server it is talking to first: gf_agent_session_bind.  There is deliberately nothing here to add, remove, lock or unlock keys, and nothing for agent forwarding (chan.h refuses it) or for an agent server.
.PP
Framing.  A frame is a uint32 length and that many bytes, the first of which is the message type.  The reader is a push parser (HACKING): it reassembles frames from chunks of any size, checks a length as soon as its four bytes are in and before anything it announces is buffered, and fails on a length of zero or over GF_AGENT_MAX_FRAME, which is also OpenSSH's limit.  A frame that arrives whole in one chunk is delivered as a slice of that chunk and copied nowhere.
.PP
Every parser here is all or nothing: a message is checked from end to end before any callback runs, and one with bytes left over after its last field, a length that runs past the end or a count larger than the bytes could hold is GF_ERR_ENCODING.  Everything an agent sends is the peer's, and a hostile one can send anything.
.PP
Keys.  A client does not take an agent's word for what a key is: identities go through gf_key_from_blob, so the key policy (key.h) stays in one place, and what it refuses is skipped and counted.  A signature comes back as the same blob gf_key_sign makes (string algorithm name, string signature; for a security key held by the agent, the flags and counter after that), which the sign event's answer checks against the public key before anything is sent to the server.
.PP
The RSA signature flags (the draft's SSH_AGENT_RSA_SHA2_256 and SSH_AGENT_RSA_SHA2_512: GF_AGENT_RSA_SHA2_256 and _512 here) are derived from the algorithm, and the SHA\-1 flag (1) is never sent: an agent asked for neither would sign with SHA\-1.
.SH "TYPES AND CONSTANTS"
.PP
.nf
#define GF_AGENT_MAX_FRAME (256u * 1024)
.fi
.RS
The longest frame (the type and its contents, not the length field) that is read or written.
.RE
.PP
.nf
#define GF_AGENT_RSA_SHA2_256 2u
.fi
.RS
Signature flags of SIGN_REQUEST.  There is none for SHA\-1, which is never asked for.
.RE
.PP
.nf
#define GF_AGENT_RSA_SHA2_512 4u
.fi
.PP
.nf
#define GF_AGENT_SIGN_OVERHEAD 17
.fi
.RS
Bytes that a SIGN_REQUEST frame has besides the key blob and the data (length, type, two string lengths, flags), and that a session\-bind frame has besides the host key, the session identifier and the signature (length, type, four string lengths, the extension's name and the forwarding flag). CAP of the functions below needs to be at least the sum.
.RE
.PP
.nf
#define GF_AGENT_BIND_OVERHEAD 46
.fi
