int \fBgf_agent_reader_feed\fP (gf_agent_reader *r, const uint8_t *data, size_t len, gf_agent_frame_cb frame_cb, void *user);
Give the reader the next LEN bytes the socket delivered (any number, in any chunks), and call FRAME_CB for each frame completed by them, in order.  All of DATA is consumed.  Returns:
.PP
.nf
.RS
GF_OK           every frame in DATA was delivered; the reader may be
                in the middle of the next (see gf_agent_reader_idle)
GF_ERR_ENCODING a length of zero
GF_ERR_RANGE    a length over GF_AGENT_MAX_FRAME
GF_ERR_NOMEM    no room to hold a partial frame
GF_ERR_HANDLER  FRAME_CB returned nonzero
GF_ERR_STATE    the reader has failed before
.RE
.fi
.PP
After any error the reader is failed for good: the stream cannot be resynchronised, and the connection to the agent should be closed.
.TP
int \fBgf_agent_reader_idle\fP (const gf_agent_reader *r);
1 if the reader is between frames, 0 if it holds part of one.  The socket ending while it does not is a truncated reply.
.SS "Requests"
.PP
Each writes a whole frame, length field included, to OUT (CAP bytes), and sets *LEN to its size.  GF_ERR_BUFSIZE if CAP is too small, and then nothing useful is in OUT.
.TP
int \fBgf_agent_request_identities\fP (uint8_t *out, size_t cap, size_t *len);
.TP
int \fBgf_agent_sign_request\fP (const gf_key *key, enum gf_sigalg alg, const uint8_t *data, size_t datalen, uint8_t *out, size_t cap, size_t *len);
Ask the agent to sign DATA (DATALEN bytes) with KEY under ALG.  The key is named by its blob (gf_key_blob: the certificate for a certificate), so KEY needs no private part; ALG must belong to it (gf_key_sigalgs, or the certificate algorithm for a certificate, or a security key's).  The flags are derived from ALG: rsa\-sha2\-256 and rsa\-sha2\-512 and their certificate forms give GF_AGENT_RSA_SHA2_256 and _512, the others 0.
.PP
GF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED for an ALG that is none of those or that policy.h refuses (there is no SHA\-1 algorithm here; 0 is not one), that is X.509 (an agent does not hold those) or that does not belong to KEY.  GF_ERR_RANGE if the frame would be over GF_AGENT_MAX_FRAME.
.SS "session\-bind@openssh.com"
.PP
An agent that holds a key with destination constraints (ssh\-add \-h) signs for it only for a session it has been told about, and checks that the host key it is told about is the one that signed the session identifier.  Send this once the host key is trusted and before asking for a signature, with the host key (gf_conn_info.host_key), the session identifier and the signature the server made over it in the first key exchange (gf_conn_info.host_sig).  It is made with the forwarding flag clear: this library does not forward agents.
.PP
An agent that does not know the extension answers FAILURE or EXTENSION_FAILURE, which ssh ignores; one that does answers SUCCESS, or FAILURE if the signature is not the host key's over the session identifier.  GF_ERR_INVAL for an empty session identifier or signature.
.TP
int \fBgf_agent_session_bind\fP (const gf_key *hostkey, gf_slice session_id, gf_slice signature, uint8_t *out, size_t cap, size_t *len);
.SS "Replies"
.PP
These read a frame's payload, as the reader delivers it.
.TP
enum \fBgf_agent_type\fP gf_agent_type (const uint8_t *payload, size_t len);
The type of a message, or GF_AGENT_UNKNOWN for one that is none of the above, one that is empty, and one of the types that carry nothing (FAILURE, SUCCESS, REQUEST_IDENTITIES, EXTENSION_FAILURE) with anything after the type byte.  The contents of the others are for their own parsers to check.
.TP
int \fBgf_agent_identities\fP (const uint8_t *payload, size_t len, gf_agent_identity_cb identity_cb, void *user, size_t *skipped);
Read an IDENTITIES_ANSWER and call IDENTITY_CB for each identity whose key gf_key_from_blob accepts, in the agent's order.  Those it refuses (DSA, ECDSA over P\-521, RSA under GF_MIN_RSA_BITS, a certificate with a bad CA signature, a blob that is malformed) are skipped and counted in *SKIPPED (which may be NULL).  GF_ERR_ENCODING for a message that is malformed (then nothing is called), GF_ERR_PROTOCOL if it is another type, GF_ERR_NOMEM, GF_ERR_HANDLER if the callback stopped.
.TP
int \fBgf_agent_sign_response\fP (const uint8_t *payload, size_t len, gf_slice *sig);
Read a SIGN_RESPONSE.  *SIG is the signature blob, a slice of PAYLOAD, for gf_auth_client_sign_respond or gf_session_auth_sign_respond, which check it against the public key.  GF_ERR_ENCODING if the message is malformed, GF_ERR_PROTOCOL if it is another type (a FAILURE is the agent refusing: use gf_agent_type to tell).
